  <revision rev="3" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>4eb0834ec9796821d0a6535c16041253</srcmd5>
    <version>0.4.0</version>
    <time>1786619796</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Update to 0.4.0

Supersedes SR 1370889, which packaged the new shell completions in the main
package. They are now split into bugwarden-bash-completion,
bugwarden-zsh-completion and bugwarden-fish-completion, each noarch, each
requiring the matching base version and carrying a Supplements so a shell's
completions are only pulled in where that shell is installed. Nothing else
changed from that request.

Upstream 0.4.0. The user-visible additions are product and field discovery
tools, support for custom fields when filing a bug, and --allowed-hosts to
restrict the inbound Host header on the HTTP transport.

Two changes alter behaviour rather than adding to it. The server now fails
startup when it cannot resolve an identity, and that identity comes from a
declared, verified login instead of the local account, so a deployment no
longer depends on whoami. The server also stops imposing one Bugzilla
instance's status workflow on every instance.

Correctness fixes worth naming: a capped search page is no longer read as the
end of results, suppressed_count is no longer under-reported as a maximum,
the HTTP body cap is derived from the attachment ceiling, and the example
policy no longer ships a rule that depended on whoami. The guard now reserves
the rule names it decides under.

Upstream also generates a man page and the three completion scripts from the
command line definition and ships them prebuilt in the release tarball, so
they are installed directly with no generation step at build time.

Verified on aarch64: build green, full test suite passes, rpmlint 0 errors /
0 warnings / 0 badness across all four binary packages, and each completion
lands in the correct subpackage with the main package carrying none of them.

One thing worth knowing rather than hiding: an earlier build of this version
hit a flaky unit test,
config::tests::startup_log_states_mode_and_source_never_key_material_i12,
which asserts on captured tracing output. Its helper installs a thread-local
subscriber and then reads a shared buffer, so under parallel test execution
the buffer can come back empty and the assertion fails with no useful
message. It passed alone, passed with --test-threads=1, and has passed on
every rebuild since. Reported upstream as plusky/bugwarden#92. No downstream
workaround was added, because the fix belongs in the test helper.</comment>
    <requestid>1370891</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="4" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>f6214dc54d114ffcfcfc2690984ae1eb</srcmd5>
    <version>0.5.0</version>
    <time>1787155173</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>- Update to 0.5.0:
  * Let the environment set allowed hosts and the auth header
  * Require a bearer token on the HTTP transport
  * Export audit records and diagnostics to an OTLP collector
  * Handle SIGTERM so container stop is graceful
  * Refuse unparsable allowed-hosts at startup
  * Normalize tool schemas for Gemini/Vertex clients, and recurse
    portable_schema into all draft-2020-12 positions
- Vendored h2 bumped to 0.4.16 for RUSTSEC-2026-0258 /
  GHSA-q83h-524g-xf6h (h2 unbounded empty DATA frames lead to
  denial of service)
- Raise the rust BuildRequires floor from 1.85 to 1.88 to match
  the workspace rust-version
- Refresh License: drop CDLA-Permissive-2.0, the
  LGPL-2.1-or-later option, the Zlib option and the
  LLVM-exception option, none of which remain in the linked
  crate set
- Ship the worked OpenTelemetry collector example as documentation</comment>
